  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through next Monday)\nIssued at 1110 PM CDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nNo significant changes for the Tuesday/Wednesday timeframe. Much \nwarmer than normal (10-20 degrees) with highs in the upper 80s to \nupper 90s each day and southerly winds. \n\nTuesday night, upper level southwesterly flow will increase \nacross the area due to the upper level low moving east through the\ncentral Rockies. Models continue to generate precipitation within\nthis faster upper level flow. Tuesday night, most of this \nprecipitation is expected to occur west of our area from the Big \nBend, northeast toward Childress. As the upper level low continues\neastward, the western half of our area may see some scattered \nshowers and thunderstorms Wednesday into Wednesday night, \nespecially if any leftover outflow boundaries can make it into our\narea from the previous day's activity. \n\nHowever, the best chance for west-central Texas to see beneficial\nrainfall is expected from Thursday afternoon into Thursday night \nas a cold front moves into the area and provides a well-defined \nfocus for convection to develop off of. Rain chances west of a \nBrownwood to Sonora line Thursday into Thursday night range from \n50%-80%. With plentiful Gulf moisture available, precipitation \ntotals in some locations could exceed 2 inches, but most areas \nwill see less than that. \n\nPrecipitation is expected to move out of the area by Friday \nevening, with much cooler temperatures Friday through the weekend.\nHighs will range from the mid 60s to around 70 Friday and \nSaturday, with temperatures warming into mainly the 70s for \nSunday. Lows will also be much cooler dropping into the 40s Friday\nand Saturday nights.",
